{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the problem, we need to find the number of non-congruent right triangles where one of the legs is 162 and the other leg and the hypotenuse are positive integers. Let's denote the legs of the right triangle as \\(162\\) and \\(b\\), and the hypotenuse as \\(c\\). By the Pythagorean theorem, we have:\n\n\\[162^2 + b^2 = c^2.\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[b^2 = c^2 - 162^2 = (c-162)(c+162).\\]\n\nWe need to find pairs of factors \\((m, n)\\) such that \\(m = c-162\\) and \\(n = c+162\\) and \\(m \\cdot n = b^2\\). Since \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are factors of \\(b^2\\) and \\(n = m + 324\\), \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) must be of the same parity (both odd or both even). Since \\(162\\) is even, \\(c\\) must be even, so \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are both even. We can set \\(m = 2k\\) and \\(n = 2l\\), and the equation becomes:\n\n\\[4kl = b^2 \\implies kl = \\left(\\frac{b}{2}\\right)^2.\\]\n\nSo, \\(kl = \\left(\\frac{b}{2}\\right)^2\\) and \\(n = m + 324 \\implies 2l = 2k + 324 \\implies l = k + 162\\). Therefore, we need to find pairs \\((k, k+162)\\) such that \\(k(k+162)\\) is a perfect square. Let's denote \\(k(k+162) = t^2\\). We need to find all such \\(k\\) such that \\(k(k+162)\\) is a perfect square.\n\n","prefix_end":1146,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,3491,11,582,1184,311,1477,279,1372,315,2477,14859,901,11680,1290,42446,1380,825,315,279,14201,374,220,16,21,17,323,279,1008,2472,323,279,9751,65628,810,525,6785,25780,13,6771,594,78064,279,14201,315,279,1290,21495,438,17767,16,21,17,57758,323,17767,65,59,701,323,279,9751,65628,810,438,17767,66,59,568,3216,279,5355,95362,45195,57817,11,582,614,1447,78045,16,21,17,61,17,488,293,61,17,284,272,61,17,7110,2533,1986,646,387,58524,438,1447,78045,65,61,17,284,272,61,17,481,220,16,21,17,61,17,284,320,66,12,16,21,17,2376,66,10,16,21,17,72341,2533,16